                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Pixel Puzzles Mosaics is a really simple puzzle game, but I actually like how different it feels from a normal jigsaw. Instead of having a pile of loose pieces that I have to connect together, the whole mosaic is already laid out in front of me and I have to rearrange the mixed up tiles until the picture is correct. There are 34 mosaics in total, with different sizes and photographs to work through. I like that the basic idea is easy to understand straight away, so I can start solving without having to learn a complicated system.\n\nThe actual puzzle design works well for me because I am looking at the whole image while trying to work out where each tile belongs. It feels more organised than a traditional jigsaw, and I can concentrate on colours, shapes and patterns rather than constantly searching around the screen for a piece that might fit. The larger mosaics can still take some patience, especially when there are similar looking parts of an image, but I think that is where most of the enjoyment comes from. I am slowly making sense of the picture rather than simply clicking through a list of objectives.\n\nI also like the relaxing atmosphere. There is no combat, timer or complicated progression getting in the way, so I can just sit down and solve a few puzzles at my own pace. The photographs give each mosaic something different to look at, and the interactive presentation makes the game feel a little more lively than simply opening a picture and putting tiles together. There are also 34 Steam achievements, which gives me something extra to work towards if I want to complete everything.\n\nThe biggest weakness for me is that the gameplay does not really evolve much. I am still rearranging tiles from beginning to end, so once I understand the system there are not many new mechanics waiting for me. That is not necessarily a problem for this type of game, but it does mean I have to genuinely enjoy the puzzle itself because there is not much else keeping me interested. The difficulty mostly comes from the size and detail of the mosaics rather than the game introducing more complicated rules.\n\nI also think the presentation is starting to show its age. Pixel Puzzles Mosaics originally released in 2017, and while the photographs themselves can look nice, the interface and overall presentation are fairly basic. There are also some old technical issues that can show up depending on how I run the game, including problems with the mouse reaching the full screen area at higher resolutions. That is not something that ruins the puzzles for me, but it can make the experience feel less polished than it could be.\n\nWhat helps a lot is that the game is free, single player and does not need a powerful computer. I can simply open it when I want something quiet and spend some time solving pictures without worrying about progression, complicated controls or a long story. The 34 mosaics give me a reasonable amount to work through, although I would have liked more variety in how the puzzles actually function. For me, it is best used as a relaxing game that I can play in short sessions rather than something I am going to sit with for hours at a time.\n\nOverall, Pixel Puzzles Mosaics is a straightforward and relaxing puzzle game that does exactly what it sets out to do. I like the mosaic style, the photographs, the simple controls and the fact that the entire game can be played at my own pace. The lack of deeper mechanics and the basic presentation keep it from reaching the higher ratings I give to games with more variety, but I still think there is enough here to make it worthwhile. It is free, easy to pick up and has enough puzzles to keep me occupied when I just want something calm to work through.\n\nRating: 7.5/10\n",
